# ── 3. True/False (50) ──
tfs = [
    ("Two cats found a roti lying on the street.","True","Easy"),("The cats were enemies before finding the roti.","False","Easy"),("The cats decided to share the roti equally.","True","Easy"),("Both cats agreed that the roti was divided perfectly.","False","Easy"),("The first cat said the second got the bigger part.","True","Easy"),("The cats chose a dog as their judge.","False","Easy"),("A monkey was sitting on a wall nearby.","True","Easy"),("The monkey had not seen the cats' fight at all.","False","Easy"),("The monkey wanted to help the cats fairly.","False","Easy"),("The monkey was looking for an opportunity to grab the roti.","True","Easy"),("The monkey lifted both parts of the roti.","True","Easy"),("The monkey used a weighing scale to measure the pieces.","False","Easy"),("The monkey took bites from the roti claiming each piece was bigger.","True","Easy"),("The cats fought the monkey and took back their roti.","False","Easy"),("The monkey gobbled up the entire roti.","True","Easy"),("The cats got at least a small bite each.","False","Easy"),("The monkey vanished after eating all the roti.","True","Easy"),("The cats learned they were foolish to fight and trust a stranger.","True","Easy"),("The moral is 'Fighting allows others to take advantage of you'.","True","Easy"),("'Frolicking' means to fight aggressively.","False","Easy"),("'Neutral' means not supporting either side.","True","Easy"),("The roti was found in a river.","False","Easy"),("The monkey pretended to be a fair judge.","True","Easy"),("The cats shared the roti happily in the end.","False","Easy"),("Chapter 03 is a Panchatantra Tale.","True","Easy"),
    ("The monkey exploited the cats' internal dispute for personal gain.","True","Medium"),("The cats could have avoided loss by compromising instead of fighting.","True","Medium"),("Internal conflict strengthens a group against outside threats.","False","Medium"),("The monkey was genuinely neutral and fair in his judgement.","False","Medium"),("Trusting strangers with personal disputes always leads to fair outcomes.","False","Medium"),("The story teaches that unity is more valuable than material possessions.","True","Medium"),("A small disagreement between the cats escalated into total loss.","True","Medium"),("The monkey's method of comparing pieces was honest and transparent.","False","Medium"),("The proverb 'United we stand, divided we fall' matches this story's moral.","True","Medium"),("The cats voluntarily gave up their decision-making power to the monkey.","True","Medium"),("Resolving disputes internally is always worse than involving outsiders.","False","Medium"),("The cats demonstrated good emotional intelligence by fighting over the roti.","False","Medium"),("The monkey showed that outsiders can exploit internal divisions.","True","Medium"),("The story has no relevance to modern human relationships.","False","Medium"),("Compromise would have saved the cats from losing their entire roti.","True","Medium"),
    ("The monkey represents opportunistic self-interest in the story.","True","Hard"),("The story functions as a political allegory about exploitation of divided groups.","True","Hard"),("Chapters 01-03 together build the framework: Think → Verify → Unite.","True","Hard"),("The monkey's vanishing symbolises the shamelessness of opportunistic exploitation.","True","Hard"),("The story encourages children to always fight over food.","False","Hard"),("The Panchatantra's moral about internal division is irrelevant in the 21st century.","False","Hard"),("Third-party mediators should always be chosen for integrity, not convenience.","True","Hard"),("The story develops emotional intelligence skills in young readers.","True","Hard"),("The cats' helplessness during the monkey's feast shows loss of agency due to ceded authority.","True","Hard"),("Chapter 03 integrates moral reasoning, vocabulary, and social-emotional learning.","True","Hard"),
]
