# ── 5. Long Answer (50) ──
las = [
    ("Narrate the complete story of 'Two Cats and the Monkey' in your own words.","One day, two friendly cats were playing on the road when they found a roti. They decided to share it equally but started arguing about who got the bigger piece. Unable to resolve the dispute, they asked a monkey sitting on a wall to be their judge.\n\nThe cunning monkey had been watching their fight and saw an opportunity. He pretended to compare the pieces, taking a bite from one saying it was bigger, then from the other. He kept repeating this until the entire roti was gone. The helpless cats could only watch. The monkey jumped on the wall and vanished. The cats realised they were foolish to fight and trust a stranger. Moral: Fighting amongst yourselves allows others to take advantage of you.","Easy","Remembering"),
    ("Describe the characters of the two cats and explain their mistake.","The two cats were friends who found a roti and tried to share it equally. However, they were stubborn and distrustful — each thought the other got more. Their mistake was:\n1. Not trusting each other despite being friends.\n2. Fighting over a tiny difference.\n3. Choosing a stranger (monkey) as judge without verifying his intentions.\n4. Ceding all decision-making power to the monkey.","Easy","Understanding"),
    ("Explain the monkey's plan and how he executed it.","The monkey had been observing the cats' fight and saw a chance to eat their roti. His plan:\n1. Eagerly agreed to be 'neutral judge'.\n2. Lifted both pieces and pretended to compare.\n3. Took bites from each piece, always claiming one was bigger.\n4. Repeated this until the entire roti was consumed.\n5. Jumped away and vanished without remorse.","Easy","Understanding"),
    ("Explain the moral of the story with examples.","Moral: Fighting amongst yourselves allows others to take advantage.\nExamples:\n- Two siblings fighting over a toy may have it confiscated by a parent.\n- Two business partners fighting may lose their company to a competitor.\n- Two friends fighting may be manipulated by a third person seeking to benefit.","Easy","Applying"),
    ("Describe the vocabulary words: Frolicking, Neutral, and Gobbled.","1. **Frolicking**: Playing and behaving in a cheerful, lively way. The cats were frolicking on the road.\n2. **Neutral**: Not supporting either side in an argument. The cats wanted a neutral judge.\n3. **Gobbled**: Ate quickly and greedily. The monkey gobbled up the full roti.","Easy","Understanding"),
    ("Why did the cats need a judge and why was their choice poor?","They needed a judge because they could not agree on whether the roti pieces were equal. Their choice was poor because:\n1. The monkey was a stranger with no obligation to be fair.\n2. They did not verify his intentions.\n3. They gave him complete authority without oversight.","Easy","Analyzing"),
    ("What would have happened if the cats had not fought?","If they had not fought, they would have eaten their roti together happily. Even if the pieces were slightly unequal, both would have been satisfied with their share. Fighting cost them everything.","Easy","Applying"),
    ("How does the story show that small problems can become big?","A tiny size difference in the roti pieces was a small problem. But it escalated: argument → tired of arguing → seeking outside help → getting cheated → losing everything. This shows how small disputes can snowball into total loss.","Easy","Analyzing"),
    ("Write a short letter from one cat to the other after the incident.","Dear Friend,\nI am so sorry for our silly fight over the roti. Because of our argument, the cunning monkey ate everything and we got nothing. I have learned that trusting each other is more important than being right about who got the bigger piece. Let us promise to always resolve our differences peacefully from now on.\nYour friend,\nCat","Easy","Creating"),
    ("Summarise Chapter 03 in five key points.","1. Two cats found a roti and decided to share equally.\n2. They argued over who got the bigger piece.\n3. They asked a monkey to judge, who pretended to compare the pieces.\n4. The monkey ate the entire roti by taking bites from each piece.\n5. The cats learned: fighting allows others to take advantage.","Easy","Understanding"),
    ("What role does greed play in both the cats' and monkey's actions?","Cats: Greed made each want the 'bigger' piece instead of accepting what they had.\nMonkey: Greed motivated him to exploit their dispute to eat the entire roti.\nIn both cases, greed led to negative outcomes.","Easy","Analyzing"),
    ("Compare the monkey's cunning with a trickster character from another story.","Like the fox in 'The Fox and the Crow' who flatters the crow to steal the cheese, the monkey exploits the cats' weakness (their dispute) to steal their roti. Both tricksters use others' flaws against them.","Easy","Comparing"),
    ("How did the cats feel at different points in the story?","1. Happy: When they found the roti.\n2. Excited: When they decided to share.\n3. Angry: When they argued over sizes.\n4. Hopeful: When they found a 'neutral' judge.\n5. Helpless: Watching the monkey eat.\n6. Sad and foolish: Realising their mistake.","Easy","Analyzing"),
    ("Why is compromise better than seeking outside judges?","Compromise preserves relationships, ensures both parties get something, and avoids the risk of a biased or self-interested outsider taking everything.","Easy","Evaluating"),
    ("Draft a new ending where the cats solve their problem wisely.","The two cats looked at the slightly unequal pieces. 'Let me take the smaller one,' said the first cat generously. 'No, I will take the smaller one,' replied the second. They laughed and swapped pieces, enjoying their roti together. The monkey on the wall sighed and went looking for food elsewhere.","Easy","Creating"),
    ("Analyse how the monkey maintains control throughout the judging.","He maintains control by: 1) Setting himself as sole authority. 2) Holding both pieces in his hands. 3) Creating a loop of 'this one is bigger' + bite. 4) Moving too quickly for the cats to protest. 5) Continuing until nothing remains.","Medium","Analyzing"),
    ("How does trust between friends break down in this story?","Trust breaks when the cats cannot accept slight inequality. Instead of trusting each other's good intentions, they assume the worst (cheating), which escalates to involving a stranger who exploits them.","Medium","Analyzing"),
    ("Compare the moral of Ch 03 with 'A stitch in time saves nine'.","Both emphasise addressing problems early. If the cats had accepted the tiny difference immediately (stitch in time), they would have saved the entire roti (saves nine).","Medium","Comparing"),
    ("How does this story prepare children for conflict resolution?","It teaches: 1) Stay calm during disagreements. 2) Compromise is better than winning at all costs. 3) Be careful who you trust to mediate. 4) Internal resolution is safer than external intervention.","Medium","Applying"),
    ("Explain the concept of 'opportunity exploitation' using the monkey's actions.","The monkey saw the cats' internal conflict as an opportunity. He positioned himself as a helper but used his authority to exploit them for personal gain — a classic case of opportunity exploitation.","Medium","Analyzing"),
    ("How does the story illustrate the consequence of stubbornness?","Both cats stubbornly insisted the other got more. Neither would compromise. This stubbornness led them to seek external help, which resulted in total loss. Stubbornness caused them to lose what flexibility would have preserved.","Medium","Evaluating"),
    ("What makes the monkey a successful manipulator?","1) He observed the vulnerability first. 2) He offered help eagerly. 3) He created a plausible excuse (making portions equal). 4) He acted quickly. 5) He left before they could react. This is a textbook manipulation pattern.","Medium","Analyzing"),
    ("How would you advise the cats if you were their friend?","I would say: 'Friends, a small difference in size does not matter when you both get to eat. Accept the pieces as they are, enjoy your meal together, and never let a small issue destroy your friendship.'","Medium","Applying"),
    ("Discuss the theme of 'Appearances vs Reality' in the story.","Appearance: The monkey appears neutral and helpful.\nReality: He is a selfish opportunist.\nAppearance: The pieces seem unequal.\nReality: The difference was negligible.\nBoth appearances deceived the cats.","Medium","Analyzing"),
    ("How does the narrative structure create suspense?","The story builds suspense by: 1) Setting up the roti find (hope). 2) The argument (tension). 3) Finding the monkey (relief). 4) The monkey eating bite by bite (growing horror). 5) Complete loss (shock). This arc keeps readers engaged.","Medium","Analyzing"),
    ("Why is the story's setting (street, wall) significant?","The street is a public place where disputes happen openly. The wall gives the monkey a vantage point to observe — and a quick escape route. These settings enable the plot naturally.","Medium","Analyzing"),
    ("How does the Panchatantra make moral lessons memorable?","Through: 1) Relatable animal characters. 2) Clear conflict and resolution. 3) Dramatic consequences. 4) Explicit moral statement. 5) Simple language for all ages.","Medium","Evaluating"),
    ("What are the consequences of not resolving conflicts peacefully?","Loss of resources (roti), damaged relationships (friendship), exploitation by outsiders (monkey), and emotional regret (foolishness). This story demonstrates all four.","Medium","Evaluating"),
    ("Design a classroom poster based on the moral of Chapter 03.","Title: 'United We Share, Divided We Lose'\nImage: Two cats smiling while sharing roti vs. a monkey eating between fighting cats.\nMessage: 'Resolve disputes together — don't let outsiders take what belongs to you!'","Medium","Creating"),
    ("Explain how this story connects to the concept of teamwork.","Teamwork requires trust, communication, and compromise. The cats failed at all three: they didn't trust each other, communicated through arguments, and refused to compromise. Result: they lost everything.","Medium","Evaluating"),
    ("Evaluate the philosophical significance of the moral for human societies.","When groups — families, communities, nations — fight internally, external powers exploit the division. History proves this repeatedly: colonialism thrived on divided societies. The Panchatantra recognised this truth millennia ago.","Hard","Evaluating"),
    ("Analyse the story through the lens of game theory.","In game theory, the cats face a cooperation vs. competition dilemma. Cooperation (sharing) yields a moderate reward for both. Competition (fighting) yields zero for both and maximum for the monkey (defector). The cats chose competition and lost.","Hard","Analyzing"),
    ("Compare the monkey's role to that of corrupt intermediaries in society.","Corrupt intermediaries (lawyers, middlemen, corrupt officials) exploit disputes between parties for personal gain — exactly as the monkey exploited the cats' fight. Both profit from others' conflicts.","Hard","Evaluating"),
    ("Synthesise how Chapters 01-03 create a complete decision-making framework.","Ch01: Think before acting (foresight).\nCh02: Gather information before acting (evidence).\nCh03: Resolve disputes internally (unity).\nTogether: Wisdom = Foresight + Evidence + Unity — a comprehensive framework for young learners.","Hard","Synthesizing"),
    ("Critique the narrative from a feminist perspective.","While the cats' gender is unspecified in the original, the story universally teaches conflict resolution regardless of gender. The moral about exploitation through division is applicable to all.","Hard","Evaluating"),
    ("Design a debate topic for Class 3 students based on this chapter.","Topic: 'Should you always solve problems with friends by yourselves, or is it okay to ask someone else to help?'\nTeam A: Internal resolution preserves trust.\nTeam B: Sometimes external mediators bring fairness.\nDebrief: Discuss when each approach works best.","Hard","Creating"),
    ("Assess the story's effectiveness in teaching emotional regulation.","Highly effective: It shows that uncontrolled anger (fighting over roti) leads to exploitation. Emotional regulation (staying calm, compromising) would have preserved both the roti and the friendship.","Hard","Evaluating"),
    ("How does the story model consequential thinking for children?","It shows: Action (fighting) → Consequence (seeking judge) → Further Consequence (exploitation) → Final Consequence (total loss). Children learn to trace action-consequence chains.","Hard","Analyzing"),
    ("Formulate a comprehensive moral lesson combining all three chapters.","Think carefully (Ch01), investigate thoroughly (Ch02), and stand united (Ch03). Never let impulsiveness, ignorance, or internal conflict create opportunities for others to exploit you.","Hard","Creating"),
    ("Write a book review of Chapter 03 for a school magazine.","'Two Cats and the Monkey' is a brilliantly crafted Panchatantra tale about two cats who lose their entire roti to a cunning monkey because of a petty argument. With vivid characters and a powerful moral about unity, it teaches young readers that fighting amongst friends only benefits outsiders. A timeless lesson in every bite — literally!","Hard","Creating"),
]
